.TH GJXDIFF 1 "2026-08-07" "gjxdiff 0.8.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gjxdiff \- structural diff for JSON and NDJSON files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gjxdiff
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.I FILE_A
.I FILE_B
.br
.B gjxdiff
.B \-\-about
.br
.B gjxdiff
.B \-\-credits
.br
.B gjxdiff
.B \-\-completions
.I SHELL
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gjxdiff
compares two JSON or NDJSON files structurally and reports the
differences. It is built for files of any size: inputs are memory-mapped,
never loaded into RAM; working state lives in temporary sidecar files
under a memory budget; diff records spool to disk as they are found \(em
never accumulated in memory \(em and the report is written out when the
comparison completes. The same inputs and flags produce a bit-identical report,
on this machine and on any other: the default memory budget is a fixed
4 GiB, so no internal limit \(em and therefore nothing in the report
\(em depends on how much RAM happens to be free.
.PP
The input format is detected per file (extension and content); a JSON
file can be compared against an NDJSON file. NDJSON files are compared
record by record: arrays of records with a detected (or manually set)
key are matched by key value, order-insensitively \(em a pure reorder
produces no records but still exits 1, disclosed as an order-only
difference. However a keyed comparison ends, stderr states how many
records must move to reconcile the two record orders, and
.B \-\-record\-order
lists them as move pairs. Renames and moved subtrees are detected and
reported as their own record kinds.
.PP
Output mode is selected git-style: when stdout is a terminal, a
color-coded human view is rendered; when stdout is piped or redirected,
the machine NDJSON report is emitted (byte-identical regardless of
flags).
.BR \-\-human ,
.B \-\-ndjson
and
.B \-\-stat
force a mode. Progress and every disclosure note go to stderr. All
stderr text passes through a terminal-safety escaping pass: bytes
derived from file content or argv (raw ESC, other C0/C1 controls,
bidirectional override code points) are escaped visibly and can never
inject terminal control sequences.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I FILE_A FILE_B
The two inputs. Required for every comparison; only the print-and-exit
flags
.RB ( \-\-about ,
.BR \-\-credits ", " \-\-completions )
run without them. Passing
.B \-
for ONE of them reads that side from standard input: the stream is
buffered to a temporary file under
.B \-\-temp
first (removed with the run directory \(em see \fB\-\-temp\fR), its format is detected
from the content alone (stdin has no file name, so the .ndjson/.jsonl
extension shortcut never applies), and the report and every message
name that input "\-". Both sides cannot be "\-" (a stream can only be
read once), and an interactive terminal on stdin is refused with exit 2
instead of waiting for typed input. While stdin is buffered the
progress line shows bytes read without a percentage (the total is
unknown until the pipe ends). "\-" composes with
.B "\-\-patch \-"
(stdin and stdout are different streams).
.IP
An input that is not a regular file \(em a process substitution
.RB ( "gjxdiff <(cmd) <(cmd)" ),
a named pipe, a socket, a device \(em is a stream, and takes the same
route: it is copied into the run's temporary directory before the
comparison, and the copy counts against
.B \-\-max\-temp
like every other temporary byte (two 3 GB streams therefore need room
for 6 GB of copies, where two regular files need none). Both sides may
be streams; both are read before either is indexed, so a producer
feeding one side is not left blocked while the other is compared. Every
message names the input the way it was typed \(em the copy's path never
appears. Reading a stream is interruptible (Ctrl\-C exits 130 and
removes the partial copy); a stream that never ends is stopped by
.B \-\-max\-temp
if one is set. Regular files are never copied.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress all output; the exit code alone answers.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-diffs " N"
Emit at most
.I N
diff records. This truncates the OUTPUT only \(em the full comparison
still runs and no work is skipped: the exit code, totals and summary
reflect the complete diff, and the summary discloses the truncation.
Conflicts with
.BR \-\-patch .
.TP
.BI \-\-memory\-limit " SIZE"
The memory budget: the anonymous memory (heap) the engine may plan for.
Accepts bytes or a K/M/G suffix (KiB/MiB/GiB, case-insensitive), e.g.
512M, 2G. Default: a fixed 4 GiB on every machine, so the budget \(em
and every cap derived from it \(em is the same number everywhere, and
the report does not depend on the machine's momentary state. An
explicit value is used verbatim: nothing clamps, raises or reinterprets
it in either direction, including above physical RAM. The budget covers
heap memory only; memory-mapped file pages sit outside it, because the
kernel reclaims them under pressure rather than failing an allocation.
Inside a container they are still charged to the container's memory
limit alongside the heap, and the physical guard below accounts for
that. The
engine's internal pool sizes (alignment cap, work stack, move
candidates, memos) scale with the budget, so a larger
limit buys item-exact alignment of larger structures, not just later
aborts; a smaller one coarsens sooner.
.IP
The fixed default trades precision for reproducibility, and on a large
machine it is worth raising. The default resolves the same limits on a
laptop and on a 64 GB build server \(em 6710886 children aligned
exactly per container, 3919580 move candidates per side \(em because
that is what makes a default report reproducible everywhere. The tool
does not help itself to memory you have; raising the budget is how you
spend it. With
.BR "\-\-memory\-limit 32G"
the alignment cap becomes 53687091 and the move pool 31356644 per side,
so structures the default coarsens into one region_changed are
reported element by element. Every limit that actually bit is
disclosed on stderr and echoed in the meta line, so a coarsened run
always tells you a larger budget would have said more.
.IP
A separate PHYSICAL GUARD watches what the run can actually get. It
aborts when anonymous memory passes the budget or 85% of MemAvailable
at launch, whichever is lower, and \(em separately \(em when the
GROWTH of a container's memory use since launch passes 85% of the room
that was free under that container's limit at launch. The container
check covers cgroup v1 and cgroup v2, every ancestor cgroup (a parent
can be tighter than your own), and every mounted view of the
hierarchy. Growth is measured live, because inside a container the
run's memory-mapped input and its temporary files are charged to the
same limit as its heap \(em and so is every other process in the
cgroup, so a neighbour growing into the remaining room still ends in
this tool's clean abort rather than a kernel kill. What a neighbour
already held at launch is subtracted first: a container mostly full of
someone else does not refuse a comparison that fits in the room that
is left. Page cache the kernel can reclaim is not counted, so merely
reading a large file in a container never triggers it.
.IP
.B memory.max
always counts.
.B memory.high
throttles instead of killing, and counts as a limit only from the
moment nothing is left to reclaim into: when the cgroup cannot swap at
all (\fBmemory.swap.max\fR 0, or no swap on the machine) it counts
from the start, and when the cgroup can swap it counts from the moment
the swap allowance is effectively exhausted \(em until then the
throttle merely slows the run down and it still finishes, while past
that point the throttle stalls the run indefinitely and a prompt abort
is the only useful outcome. v1's
.B memory.soft_limit_in_bytes
is not a limit for this purpose \(em it only biases reclaim priority
under machine-wide pressure \(em and is deliberately ignored.
.IP
The guard can only stop a run \(em it never changes a cap and never
changes output, so a machine or container smaller than the budget
still produces byte-identical results for every run that fits. When it
trips, the message names which number bound the run, reports the
figures, and says what to do:
.RS
.PP
.EX
gjxdiff: machine memory exhausted (rss 1.8 GiB > physical guard 1.7 GiB); partial temp files removed
gjxdiff: the machine bound this run \(em the guard is 85% of MemAvailable at launch (1.7 GiB), below the 4 GiB memory budget; free memory on this machine, or pass a smaller --memory-limit (a smaller budget may coarsen large arrays and objects instead of aligning them exactly)

gjxdiff: container memory limit reached (growth since launch 679.8 MiB > container guard 679.6 MiB); partial temp files removed
gjxdiff: a container memory limit bound this run \(em the cgroup v2 limit is 800 MiB, 523.4 KiB of it was in use at launch, and the guard stops the run at 85% of the 799.5 MiB that was free (679.6 MiB), below the 4 GiB memory budget; reclaimable page cache is not counted; raise the container's memory limit, free memory in the container, or pass a smaller --memory-limit (a smaller budget may coarsen large arrays and objects instead of aligning them exactly)
.EE
.RE
.IP
In a container so tight that the run cannot even unwind in the room
left above the guard, a third line reports that the run was ended
immediately instead. The exit code, the message and the temp-file
cleanup are the same; only the graceful unwind is skipped. That makes
a clean early exit overwhelmingly likely, but it is not a guarantee:
in a narrow band of container limits the kernel's OOM killer can still
win the race against the abort itself, in which case the run ends with
the kernel's kill (exit code 137) and temporary files may be left
behind \(em the next start's sweep removes them.
.IP
If neither /proc/meminfo nor any cgroup limit can be read the guard has
no physical component and falls back to the budget alone. A cgroup
limit that is readable while its usage file is not (a masked or
restricted /sys/fs/cgroup view) is watched through the nearest parent
cgroup whose usage is readable \(em which may abort the run earlier
than the limit alone requires \(em and a warning at launch says so.
The room the guard shares out is then the limit minus everything that
parent already held at launch, which can be far smaller than what the
limit alone would leave \(em down to nothing, when the parent already
held more than the limit \(em so a run that would fit under the limit
itself can still be refused. The guard also depends on the usage file
it watches staying readable for the whole run: one that turns
unreadable mid-run and stays that way leaves the limit unwatched from
that point on, without a further message. If
no usage file is readable at any level, the warning states instead
that the limit cannot be watched at all, and if the container then
runs out of memory the kernel ends the run with no message and
temporary files may be left behind (a later run with the same
\fB\-\-temp\fR removes them). These warnings print even under
\fB\-\-quiet\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-temp " DIR"
Root directory for temporary files (default: the system temp dir). Run
data lives in
.IR DIR /gjxdiff/run\-<pid>\-<nonce>,
removed on exit \(em including on Ctrl-C. Two narrow cases can outrun
the cleanup: a second SIGINT/SIGTERM within the shutdown's first
milliseconds, and a kernel out-of-memory kill inside a container too
small for the workload while an abort is already under way; whatever
remains \(em like directories left by killed runs \(em is swept at the
next start. Created if missing; the
.IR DIR /gjxdiff
container is removed again when it is left empty (never while another
run's data is inside).
.TP
.BI \-\-align\-cap " N|unlimited"
Max direct children per container aligned exactly; a larger container
degrades to one coarse "region_changed" record, disclosed on stderr. A
positive count, or "unlimited" to align any size (memory is then
governed by the memory budget alone). Default: scaled from the memory
budget as budget/640, never below 500000 \(em with the fixed 4 GiB
default budget that is exactly 6710886 children, the same number on
every machine and in every run. On a run where an over-cap container
was actually encountered, the resolved value is disclosed in a stderr
note (every mode) and echoed in the meta line's "align_cap" filter key
(see REPORT FORMAT). Raising the cap also raises the work-stack limit
to twice the cap; the move-candidate pool is not affected by this flag
\(em it follows the budget, so
.B \-\-memory\-limit
is the knob for it.
.TP
.BI \-\-large\-arrays " MODE"
How containers larger than the alignment cap are handled.
.B coarse
(the default, and the only mode available in this version) degrades
each over-cap divergent region to one coarse "region_changed" record,
disclosed on stderr.
.B exact
\(em a disk-spilled exact path with no cap \(em is PLANNED for a future
version; passing it today exits 2 with a clear message. The active mode
is always echoed in the meta line's "large_arrays" filter key.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-temp " SIZE"
Abort when the run's temporary files (sidecars, spools, the result
file \(em everything under this run's temp directory) exceed this total
size. Same size grammar as
.B \-\-memory\-limit
(bytes or a K/M/G suffix). No default: temp use is unlimited unless
set. Enforcement is sampled several times a second rather than checked
on every write, so a run that spools quickly can pass the cap by what
it wrote within one sampling interval before it is stopped. Measured
across varied corpora, peak temp use ranged from roughly 0.15x to 2.1x
of the two inputs' combined size, driven mainly by how many records
and nodes the inputs pack per byte \(em many tiny records land near
the high end, fewer larger records near the low end \(em with the
amount of difference a secondary factor. There is no reliable rule
from input size alone \(em the cap is a backstop against runaway disk
use, not a meter. Size one by measuring an uncapped run on
representative inputs, then keep generous headroom.
Exceeding the budget is a clean exit 2 ("temp budget exceeded") with
every partial temp file removed.
.TP
.BI \-\-key " FIELDS"
Record identity for array pairing: comma-separated field names that
jointly identify a record (compound key, at most 16 fields). Repeatable;
occurrences accumulate ("\-\-key region \-\-key id" =
"\-\-key region,id"). One level of nesting via a dot ("meta.id"); "\\,"
escapes a literal comma in a field name. Fields are forced globally:
arrays where they resolve are matched by them, others fall back to
autodetection, then order-based alignment. "\-\-key none" (alone)
disables keyed matching entirely. Default: autodetect. Every keyed
pairing, rejected key and quality degradation is disclosed on stderr.
.IP
Records sharing a key value pair in the order they appear, which may
pair the wrong two. An AUTODETECTED key is therefore re-checked against
every record of the compared range once the pairing is done and before
anything is reported (detection itself only samples): below 99% distinct
values it is dropped, the array falls back to order-based alignment, and
the reason is named ("key candidate ... rejected (duplicate values)").
A key that stays keeps the pairing \(em discarding a five-million-record
join over one collision reports far worse \(em and its duplicates are
disclosed by count instead ("auto key ... has duplicate values (2 of
400000 records) ... pairing quality degraded"). A forced key is never
dropped, and gets the same note reading "forced key".
.TP
.B \-\-record\-order
Also compare RECORD ORDER inside key-matched arrays, listing every
record that must move as a
.B moved_from
+
.B moved_to
pair. Keyed matching pairs records by key value and ignores where they
sit, which is what makes it useful on re-sorted exports; how many
records must move is disclosed on stderr on every run, with or without
this flag. The flag adds the records themselves to the report \(em one
pair per moved record, so a fully re-sorted array produces a pair for
nearly every record. The pairing is proven by the key, not by content,
so a record that moved AND changed produces the move pair plus its
value-level records, and the two halves may differ in content. A moved
item that is not a container is counted but not listed (a scalar is
never a move record), and that difference is disclosed. Where several
sets of records of the same size would each reconcile the order, one of
them is reported: a run is deterministic and the count is the same
either way, but comparing the same two files in the other argument
order can name a different set, and so list a different number of them.
Arrays aligned
by order \(em including everything under "\-\-key none" \(em always
compare order and are unaffected. The
.B \-\-patch
export refuses an absorbed reorder with or without this flag.
.TP
.BI \-\-ignore " PATTERNS"
Exclude fields/paths from the comparison: matching nodes are treated as
absent on BOTH sides \(em no records for them or beneath them, and
subtrees differing only in ignored content compare AND pair as
identical. Repeatable; commas split patterns ("\\," = literal comma). A
bare name ("ts") matches that member at ANY depth. Anything containing
"$", ".", "[" or "]" is an anchored path from the root: "$.meta.ts"
(the "$." is optional), "items[0].v" ([N] = exact array position,
positional only \(em key-matched records are not masked by position),
"$.arr[*].ts" ([*] = any position), "$.cfg.*" (* = any member name at
that level), ["dotted.name"] (quoted names for dots, special
characters, edge whitespace; [""] = the empty member name). Active
patterns and every masked site are disclosed on stderr; when nothing
but ignored content differs, the exit code is 0.
.TP
.BI \-\-path " PATH"
Compare only the subtree at this exact path. Both files must contain it
\(em missing on either side is an error naming the file. Report paths
are then rooted at the selected subtree ("$" = the path) and the
applied path is disclosed on stderr. Single occurrence. Anchored
address, no wildcards: "$.data.items[3].users" (the "$" is optional;
[N] = exact array position \(em on NDJSON, "$[N]" selects record N,
i.e. line N+1), ["dotted.name"] quotes names containing dots, brackets
or edge whitespace ([""] = the empty member name). A scalar target
compares as a single value. "\-\-path $" selects the whole document. A
member name duplicated at its level in either file makes the address
ambiguous \(em error; compare its parent.
.B \-\-ignore
patterns stay anchored at the ORIGINAL document root;
.B \-\-key
applies inside the subtree.
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-\-human
Human-readable view: color-coded records grouped by parent path, with
old \-> new values, ending in the stat footer. The default when stdout
is a terminal; this flag forces it even when piped.
.TP
.B \-\-ndjson
Machine NDJSON report even on a terminal (the piped/redirected default;
byte-identical either way).
.TP
.B \-\-stat
Print only the stat footer (change totals); no per-record output.
.TP
.BI \-\-color " WHEN"
When the human view uses color: "auto" = only on a terminal (the
NO_COLOR convention is respected), "always" = ANSI even piped (for
.BR "less \-R" ),
"never". The machine report is never colored. Default: auto.
.TP
.BI \-\-progress " WHEN"
Progress on stderr: "auto" = rewritten status line on a terminal only,
"always" = plain sequential lines for CI logs (no rewriting, max one
line per stage per ~2s), "never" = off.
.B \-\-quiet
overrides. Default: auto.
.TP
.BI \-\-patch " FILE"
Also write an RFC 6902 JSON Patch (one JSON array of add/remove/replace
operations) that transforms file A into file B under gjxdiff's semantic
equality. "\-" writes the patch to stdout INSTEAD of the report (the
two formats never share a stream). The file is written atomically
(temp + rename); an error or interrupt leaves nothing behind. The
export fails with exit 2 \(em never a silently partial patch \(em when
a needed record has no concrete RFC 6901 address, or when keyed pairing
absorbed a record reorder, which the patch export does not support
(rerun with "\-\-key none" for a positional patch; the error states how
many records must move). The refusal stands under
.B \-\-record\-order
too: a record that moved and changed carries value-level records
alongside its move pair, and applying both would patch it twice.
Conflicts with
.B \-\-max\-diffs
(a truncated patch would corrupt data) and
.B \-\-only
(a filtered patch would be silently partial).
.TP
.BI \-\-only " KINDS"
Emit only these record kinds (comma-separated; repeatable, occurrences
accumulate):
.BR add ", " remove ", " change ", " type
(type_changed),
.B rename
(key_renamed),
.B move
(moved_from + moved_to pairs),
.B coarse
(region_changed). Under order-based alignment a displaced item reports
as a move pair when it is an object or array of at least 64 bytes in
its own file; displaced scalars and smaller containers report as
removed+added instead. Keyed arrays absorb reorders: a record that only
changed position produces no records, so
.B \-\-only move
comes up empty there \(em however many records moved. The stderr key
note states how many must move, and
.B \-\-record\-order
puts the pairs in the report, where this filter selects them like any
other move. Only the report is filtered \(em the comparison is not
narrowed: the summary and stat footer keep the full totals plus the
suppressed count, stderr discloses the suppression per kind, the meta
line's "only" filter key echoes the active set, and the EXIT CODE
always reflects the full comparison (a run whose every difference is
suppressed still exits 1). Emitted record lines are byte-identical to
the unfiltered report's (the "i" field keeps the unfiltered index).
Conflicts with
.BR \-\-patch .
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " NAME"
Flag bundle preset, applied BEFORE explicit flags \(em an explicit flag
always overrides its profile component:
.RS
.TP
.B ci
\-\-ndjson \-\-color=never \-\-progress=never
.TP
.B quick
\-\-max\-diffs 1000 \-\-progress=auto
.TP
.B thorough
\-\-align\-cap unlimited \-\-progress=auto
.RE
.IP
Profiles never change comparison semantics silently: a limiting
component (quick's \-\-max\-diffs) is disclosed exactly like the
explicit flag would be, and the report meta line carries the resolved
component flags, never the profile name.
.B \-\-profile quick
conflicts with
.B \-\-patch
(its \-\-max\-diffs component would truncate the patch, and
\-\-max\-diffs itself cannot combine with \-\-patch); drop the profile
or drop \-\-patch.
.TP
.B \-\-about
Print the version/attribution block plus a one-line description and the
licensing contact, then exit 0. Like every print-and-exit flag
.RB ( \-\-credits ", " \-\-completions ", " \-\-help ", " \-\-version ),
it runs BEFORE run-time validation: combined with flags that would be
refused at run time (say, an unavailable mode like
.BR "\-\-large\-arrays exact" ),
it still prints and exits 0. Malformed flag syntax and unknown values
remain parse errors (exit 2) in any combination.
.TP
.B \-\-credits
Print the third-party open-source license notices (the bundled
THIRD\-PARTY\-NOTICES file), then exit 0 — the attribution travels with
the binary itself, not just the release archive.
.TP
.BI \-\-completions " SHELL"
Print a shell completion script for
.BR bash ", " zsh " or " fish
to stdout, then exit 0. The script is generated from the live flag
table, so it cannot drift from the binary. Install e.g.:
.IP
.EX
gjxdiff \-\-completions bash > /etc/bash_completion.d/gjxdiff
.EE
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help (\-h for a summary).
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and attribution block (both spellings print the same
block).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The inputs are identical: byte-identical, identical under semantic
comparison (whitespace, number and string-escape representation
normalize), or identical apart from
.BR \-\-ignore d
content.
.TP
.B 1
Differences found. This includes order-only differences (zero records)
and runs whose every record was suppressed by
.BR \-\-only .
.TP
.B 2
Error: malformed input, invalid flags, a failed patch export, or a
tripped memory or temp budget. A parse error names the file and the byte
offset of the offending byte
.RI ( FILE ": format: " MESSAGE " at byte " OFFSET ;
the offset points at the offending byte or the byte where the problem
became detectable, e.g. the opening byte of a container left unclosed
at end of input, and is omitted only where no single offending byte
exists, such as blank input). No report is emitted, with one exception:
a
.BI \-\-patch " FILE"
run whose final rename fails exits 2 with the already-streamed report
on stdout.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted (SIGINT/SIGTERM). Temporary files are removed (a second
signal within the shutdown's first milliseconds can outrun the cleanup;
the next start sweeps what remains); stdout may
carry an already-flushed prefix of the report \(em treat it as
truncated.
.SH REPORT FORMAT
The machine report (piped default, or
.BR \-\-ndjson )
is NDJSON: one JSON object per line. The FIRST line is a versioned meta
record; consumers gate on its "gjxdiff" field (report format version,
currently 1; schema stability "draft" until the v1 schema freeze).
It identifies the inputs by BASENAME only (full paths never enter the
report), carries each side's byte size and detected format, and echoes
the active comparison-narrowing flags under "filters" \(em always all
eight keys: "key", "ignore", "path", "align_cap", "move_cap",
"max_diffs", "only", "large_arrays" (null/[] when inactive;
"large_arrays" is always "coarse", the only over-cap mode that
exists). "align_cap" is the explicit flag value when one was passed;
under the default cap it is null, EXCEPT on runs that entered a
cap-dependent rail \(em an over-cap container, or a work-stack trip,
whose limit is twice the cap \(em the records then depend on the cap,
so the resolved value is echoed there (and disclosed as a stderr note
in every output mode). "move_cap" follows
the same rule for the budget-scaled move-candidate pool: null unless
the pool saturated during the run; the resolved per-side pool cap
when it did \(em moves past the cap report as plain removed+added
records, so the records depend on it (also disclosed as a stderr note
in every output mode, with a computed \-\-memory\-limit remedy).
DETERMINISM: every budget-derived limit resolves from the memory
budget alone, and the default budget is a fixed 4 GiB, so the same
inputs and flags produce the same report on every machine \(em
including the runs where a limit actually bit. Nothing here varies
with free RAM; passing
.B \-\-memory\-limit
changes the budget, and therefore may change those limits and the
records that depend on them, but any given value is as reproducible as
the default. The one thing the environment still decides is whether the
run COMPLETES: a machine or container that cannot supply the budget
aborts (see the physical guard under
.BR \-\-memory\-limit )
rather than reporting something different.
.PP
Every following line is one diff record with fields:
.PP
.EX
i          record index, 0-based, stable across runs (keeps the
           unfiltered index under --only)
op         "added" | "removed" | "changed" | "moved_from" |
           "moved_to" | "key_renamed" | "type_changed" |
           "region_changed"
path       wildcard path pattern of the change site ("$.users[*].name";
           array indices fold to [*], "$" is the root/overflow bucket).
           A member name that would collide with the grammar -- one
           containing . [ ], starting or ending with a space, the empty
           name, or a name made only of * -- is written as a quoted
           step: $["http.status_code"], $["x[*]"][*], $[""], and
           $['a"b.c'] when the name itself contains a double quote.
           That is the spelling --path and --ignore accept, so a
           printed path can be pasted back. Ordinary names are
           unchanged. A name carrying BOTH quote characters has no
           spelling in this grammar (there are no escapes): it prints
           double-quoted so the step boundaries stay visible, but only
           "ptr" addresses it losslessly
ptr        RFC 6901 JSON Pointer of the change site with CONCRETE array
           indices, addressing the record's op-side instance (file A
           for removed/moved_from, file B otherwise). Omitted -- never
           guessed -- when no reliable single-node address exists
           (duplicated member names on the chain, names that do not
           decode losslessly, pattern-table pathologies).
a_off, a_len   byte span in file A (0 for pure additions)
b_off, b_len   byte span in file B (0 for pure removals)
move_pair  only on moved_from/moved_to: the "i" of the counterpart
flags      only when non-empty: "verified" | "coarsened" | "keyed"
.EE
.PP
The pattern table behind the "path" field holds at most 65535 distinct
paths besides the root \(em a fixed capacity that does not scale with
.B \-\-memory\-limit
\(em past it, records carry the aggregated path "$" with no ptr and
one stderr note counts them.
.PP
The stderr summary counts changed (changed + key_renamed + type_changed
+ region_changed), added, deleted (the record class the report and the
stat footer call "removed"), moved (subtree pairs count once). Records
a keyed join found out of position and did not list follow the move
count as "(+N reordered, not listed)": the move count is a count of
move RECORDS, and without
.B \-\-record\-order
a keyed reorder produces none. With the flag the clause covers only the
displaced items no listing can express \(em a scalar has no node a move
record could address.
Anything the comparison skips, absorbs or coarsens \(em keyed pairing
ignoring order, ignored fields, coarsened regions, a saturated
move-candidate pool, suppressed record kinds \(em is disclosed on
stderr: the report is never silently partial.
.SH INPUT LENIENCY
gjxdiff is not an RFC 8259 validator. String contents are compared as
raw bytes: unescaped control characters, lone surrogates and invalid
UTF-8 do not abort the diff. A leading UTF-8 BOM is tolerated; in
NDJSON mode a BOM at the start of any record line is also tolerated
(stripped and disclosed on stderr). Structural errors (unclosed
containers, trailing commas, unquoted keys, non-JSON formats) are
rejected with exit 2. Flag VALUES are whitespace-trimmed before
parsing, so a member name with leading or trailing whitespace needs the
quoted form ($["name "]) in
.B \-\-path
and
.BR \-\-ignore .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Set and non-empty: disables color under \-\-color=auto (an explicit
"always" or "never" wins).
.SH EXAMPLES
Compare two files, human view on a terminal:
.IP
.EX
gjxdiff a.json b.json
.EE
.PP
CI gate \(em machine report, no color, no progress; the exit code
answers:
.IP
.EX
gjxdiff \-\-profile ci a.ndjson b.ndjson > report.ndjson
.EE
.PP
Match records by compound key, ignore volatile fields:
.IP
.EX
gjxdiff \-\-key region,id \-\-ignore ts,updated_at a.ndjson b.ndjson
.EE
.PP
Only adds and removes, as a quick content census:
.IP
.EX
gjxdiff \-\-only add,remove a.json b.json
.EE
.PP
Compare one subtree and export an RFC 6902 patch:
.IP
.EX
gjxdiff \-\-path '$.data.items' \-\-patch changes.json a.json b.json
.EE
.PP
Compare a live API response (standard input) against a saved snapshot:
.IP
.EX
curl \-s https://api.example.com/items | gjxdiff \- snapshot.json
.EE
